---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
---

<Layout title="Security" description="How Tor Browser Installer verifies downloads: SHA-256, PGP signature verification, and what it deliberately does not hide about its own limits.">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow"><Icon name="shield" size={14} /> Security</span>
      <h1>What this installer checks, and what it can't hide from you.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Downloading and running Tor Browser is security-sensitive. This page is the
        same disclosure the app makes to itself, in the code, written out
        in full rather than summarized away.
      </p>

      <div class="callout callout--warn">
        <Icon name="cross" size={20} />
        <div>
          <h4>This is not a Tor connection.</h4>
          <p>
            The installer downloads Tor Browser over <strong>plain HTTPS</strong>, not
            over the Tor network. A network observer — your ISP, a coffee-shop
            Wi-Fi operator, anyone between you and the Tor Project's servers — can see
            that this machine fetched Tor Browser, even though they can't see what
            you do inside it afterward.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 48px;">Verification process</h2>
      <ol class="verify-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Checksum verification.</strong> The downloaded file's SHA-256 hash is
          computed locally and compared against the value reported by the Tor
          Project's own release JSON. A mismatch stops the install immediately — the
          file is discarded.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>PGP signature verification.</strong> The detached <code>.asc</code>
          signature is downloaded alongside the release, and verified against the
          Tor Browser Developers signing key using <code>sequoia-openpgp</code>. This
          key is fetched at verification time from <code>keys.openpgp.org</code>.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="fingerprint-card">
        <span class="eyebrow">Tor Browser Developers key fingerprint</span>
        <p class="mono fingerprint">EF6E 286D DA85 EA2A 4BA7&nbsp;DE68 4E2C 6E87 9329 8290</p>
        <p style="margin:0; font-size:0.86rem; color:var(--app-text-secondary);">
          Compare this against the fingerprint published on
          <a href="https://support.torproject.org/tbb/how-to-verify-signature/">the Tor Project's own signature-verification guide</a>
          before trusting any build of this tool that claims to check against it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p style="margin-top:16px;">
        All verification happens <strong>before</strong> installation. If either check
        fails, nothing is copied, extracted, or installed, and no partial state is
        left behind beyond the (already-rejected) temp download, which is cleaned up.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:48px;">Elevated installs</h2>
      <p>
        A system-wide (“all users”) install writes into a location regular accounts
        don't own — <code>/Applications</code> is already system-owned on macOS,
        and <code>/opt</code> is on Linux. Rather than discover a permission failure
        partway through copying files, the app asks for a password up front and runs
        the whole install as one authenticated <code>sudo -s</code> command. On
        macOS, a per-user install additionally has a fallback: it tries a plain copy
        first, and only escalates to the native Touch ID / password prompt if that
        copy is actually refused.
      </p>
      <p>The password itself is kept only in memory, sent once to the background
        worker thread when the install starts, and is never written to the
        “View commands” log or anywhere on disk.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:48px;">Known limitation this project is upfront about</h2>
      <p>
        Earlier prototypes of this codebase shipped checksum-only verification
        without the PGP step. If you're auditing a build, confirm the binary you're
        running actually performs signature verification (search for
        <code>verify_pgp_signature</code> in <code>main.rs</code>) rather than
        assuming from this page alone — this site describes the current source,
        not whatever binary happens to be on your disk.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .narrow { max-width: 720px; }
  .callout {
    display: flex;
    gap: 14px;
    align-items: flex-start;
    padding: 18px 20px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    margin-top: 28px;
  }
  .callout--warn {
    background: rgba(200, 45, 60, 0.06);
    border: 1px solid rgba(200, 45, 60, 0.25);
    color: var(--app-error);
  }
  .callout--warn h4 { margin: 0 0 4px; color: var(--app-error); }
  .callout--warn p { margin: 0; color: #7a2129; font-size: 0.94rem; }
  .verify-list { display: grid; gap: 14px; padding-left: 22px; }
  .fingerprint-card {
    margin-top: 28px;
    background: var(--app-purple-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--app-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    padding: 20px 22px;
  }
  .fingerprint {
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    color: var(--app-purple-deep);
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    margin: 8px 0 12px;
  }
</style>
